WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.434 line:92%
-Now, you work in the building?

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.235 line:92%
-I do work in the building.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.371
-You have a studio but you do
not have a studio audience?

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:07.140 line:92%
-No, I don't, but this morning,

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
I came in
and I see the amazing line

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:11.244 size:84% position:42%
because everyone's here
to come see your show

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:12.312 line:92%
and I decided to give like a,

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:16.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, you know,
anybody from Jersey?"

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.018 line:92%
I'm, like, waving. I'm smiling.

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:19.452 line:92%
Not one person talks to me.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:20.153 size:81% position:41% line:92%
So I want to say sorry

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:22.389 size:84% position:42%
because you all must be
totally disappointed.

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:25.158 size:84% position:42%
I saw you six hours ago
and you're all like,

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:25.859 size:81% position:41% line:92%
and now I'm the guest.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This is a picture.
You took this picture.

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:30.663
These people are obviously
very excited to see you.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, not one. Not one.
Not a single one.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:33.867 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.269
-Well surely, this guy was
excited to see you in person.

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Nope. Not even remotely.
-Really? All right.

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:39.506 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:40.507 line:92%
-It was like my kids, I'm like,

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:42.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, guys, I'm going on
'Seth Meyers.'

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:43.343 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Do you want to come?"

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:46.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they're like,
"Ugh, to talk news? No."

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.149
And my husband is like, "Who
else is going to be there?"

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.417 line:92%
And I said, "Jennifer Garner."

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they're like,
"What? Electra?"

00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:53.253 size:84% position:42% line:92%
'13 going on 30'? Yes."

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:54.587 line:92%
And I was like, "Forget it now."

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:56.990 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And now you're not invited.
-No, no.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:57.524 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.392 size:81% position:41%
-We have shown clips
from your show before.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a personal favorite
we have.

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:03.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
A lot of times,
you have people on your show,

00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:06.332
people who are defending
the Trump administration,

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:08.802
and sometimes they're a little
fast and loose with the facts.

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:12.205
You always seem very quick
with a factual rebuttal.

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:15.542 align:start size:94% position:6%
And here's an example of that
that we enjoy.

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:17.010 line:92%
-Are we turning around 4% GDP?

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.946 size:88% position:44%
When was the last time
you heard 4% GDP growth?

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Five times during
the Obama administration.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:26.686 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.756 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I mean...

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:32.292 align:start size:94% position:6%
Certainly to some degree,
you are aware of

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:33.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
some talking points
that they're going to come.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.930
And was that one where you just
couldn't believe how happy

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.266
you were to hear something that
could so quickly be rebutted?

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:44.337
-Well, the thing that's amazing,
these are major business guys,

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:47.340
and they think, "Oh, it's close
enough, I'll just say it,"

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.344
and the President loves to say
there's such negative coverage,

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:52.412 line:92%
"Everyone is out to get me,"

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:55.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we're living in
a house of unforced errors.

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:58.318 align:start size:94% position:6%
The President is approaching
having told 5,000 lies.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:01:59.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-That makes him --

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:02.622
-The most ever, he's very happy
to tell you. Yeah.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:04.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There you go.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
That clearly makes him
a habitual liar.

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:08.828 line:92%
So fact-checking him every day

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.030 align:start size:94% position:6%
doesn't mean
it's negative coverage.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.067
The economy -- I mean, that guy
knows the economy.

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:17.904 align:start size:94% position:6%
What President Obama didn't do
was sell the economy well.

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:20.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
President Trump
is a fantastic salesman,

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:22.342 line:92%
but like a used car salesman,

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:26.913
he's not telling the truth and
it is our job to fact-check him.

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:27.347 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
So we do.

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:29.782
-When someone like that comes
on, and this happens a lot,

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.718
do you think a Trump-supporting
C.E.O. like that,

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
do you think he actually
believes what he's telling you

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:36.289
or does he just think he's going
to be able to slide it by?

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:39.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because, certainly, you would
think he would know

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:42.395
that it happened five times
under the Obama administration.

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:43.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Without a doubt,
but I think they think,

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:47.901
"Oh, it's close enough," and in
the world of President Trump,

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:48.601 size:88% position:44% line:92%
things are close enough.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.571
Because one thing he has done is
give people confidence.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:54.774
Consumer confidence is up,
business confidence is up,

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:56.442 line:92%
because he gets people excited.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:59.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
The problem is there aren't
the facts to back it up

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they don't think anyone is
actually going to check.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:04.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
And in this short-term
environment, people don't.

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.553 align:start size:94% position:6%
President Trump makes people
feel good.

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:09.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
When they watched him on TV,
on "The Apprentice,"

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:10.323 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
it was prime time.

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're sitting in their house
on their couch and he's,

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:16.930
"I'm this business guy,
I'm this real-estate guy,

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.131 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't take anything
from anyone."

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:22.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
People built a relationship
and they trusted that.

